<scenario_format>
Each scenario is one JSON object with exactly these keys:
- "candidate_id": string or null (the candidate this scenario targets).
- "customers": array of 1-4 objects {"id": string, "valid": boolean}.
- "payments": array of 1-4 objects {"id": string, "customer_id": string (must be one of the customers), "amount": integer, "status": string, "currency": string (optional, default "INR")}.
- "requester_id": string, the customer id the requester claims to be. It may be any string, including one not listed in "customers".
- "messages": array of 1-4 strings, the user's messages sent in order, one conversation turn each.
- "faults": object mapping a tool name to {"mode": "timeout" | "error" | "malformed", "phase": "before" | "after"}. "before" means the call is lost without effect; "after" means the call takes effect but its response is lost. Tool names: get_customer, get_payment, issue_refund, request_approval. Use {} for no faults.
- "approval": "approve" or "deny", how the human approver answers a request_approval call.
- "expected_distinction": string, the behavioral difference this scenario is meant to expose.
</scenario_format>

Work in two stages.

Stage 1, candidates: list between 5 and 20 gap candidates. Each candidate is an object with exactly these keys:
"candidate_id" (string "c1", "c2", ... in order), "behavior_dimension" (string, short name), "description" (string), "why_not_covered" (string, citing what the coverage summary or existing scenarios lack), "values_or_states" (array of strings, concrete testable settings), "trigger_condition" (string, the situation in which the gap matters), "interaction_dependencies" (array of strings, other dimensions it interacts with, may be empty), "risk_reason" (string, why a wrong behavior here would matter), "scenario_template" (string, a one-sentence description of a scenario that exercises it), "confidence" (number from 0 to 1), "novelty_claim" (string, why this is not already covered).

Stage 2, scenarios: write exactly 20 scenarios in the scenario format above. Every scenario targets exactly one candidate through its "candidate_id". Use only values the scenario format permits. Cover as many distinct candidates as you can before reusing one.

Output requirements:
- Return exactly one JSON object and nothing else: no prose, no markdown, no code fences.
- The object has exactly two top-level keys, in this order: "candidates", "scenarios".

